#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143642} :activity/id #uuid "3f180661-73e7-595c-8511-2de14736d9db" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048825 :topic/id #uuid "c3562790-cb6d-5f45-be78-dadca66e0728" :topic/math-academy-id 2398} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144071 :tutorial/content "To solve an expression that contains parentheses, $(\\,)$, we follow the **order of operations with parentheses**. This means that we calculate the operation inside parentheses first, from left to right, before following the standard order of operations.\n\nFor example, let's use the order of operations with parentheses to evaluate $30 \\div (5 \\times 2)$.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 \\div (5 \\cdot 2) &=  \\\\\n&= 30 \\div 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 \\div 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c73a1b14-33f9-5b9c-b640-581391c33369" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8566 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a6208615-4a52-50a0-9b21-85e9438bf049" :step/math-academy-id 131197 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143643}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142780 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142595 :question/id #uuid "d2914b4d-bbb5-5433-b5bb-312caff3e2c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13714"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34ea56b4-2066-5aaf-aea9-08515803fc0b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048854}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440392}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression With Parentheses"} :step/id #uuid "478d904d-1a8d-5b6d-a559-ba86c099c34f" :step/math-academy-id 131198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144072 :tutorial/content "Another pair of grouping symbols that are similar to parentheses are square brackets, $[\\:]$.\n\nTo solve an expression that contains both parentheses and brackets, the order of operations with parentheses tells us to:\n\n- Calculate the operations in the parentheses *first*.\n- Calculate the operations in square brackets *second*.\n- Finally, follow the order of operations, as usual.\n\nTo illustrate, let's evaluate the following expression:\n\n$$\n1+ [2 \\times (3 + 4)]\n$$\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + [2 \\cdot (3 + 4)] &=  \\\\\n&= 1 + [2 \\cdot 7]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + [2 \\cdot 7] &=  \\\\\n&= 1 + 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + 14 &=  \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "063858eb-73d6-58c7-9161-ad5726a4de6a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8568 :tutorial/title "Numerical Expressions With Square Brackets"} :step/id #uuid "6913fc3f-2bae-5cc2-a51c-c510bafb7078" :step/math-academy-id 131202 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142781 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142596 :question/id #uuid "947ef370-3cdf-5566-b573-b0c74d2b1424" :question/math-academy-id "e-13716"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e3f8398-2bfb-52c7-b293-3aaf8870ed39" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143211}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Containing Square Brackets"} :step/id #uuid "b526bcd5-6f3b-547a-9c4c-d149ec5cdf24" :step/math-academy-id 131203 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144073 :tutorial/content "Yet another pair of grouping symbols that are similar to parentheses and square brackets are braces, $\\{\\,\\}$.\n\nFor example, one expression that uses all three grouping symbols is:\n$\\{6 \\div [(3 + 5) \\div 4] \\} + 5$\n\nFor expressions containing parentheses, square brackets, and braces, the order of operations with parentheses tells us to:\n\n- Calculate the operations in the parentheses *first*.\n- Calculate the operations in the square brackets *second*.\n- Calculate the operations in braces *third*.\n- Finally, follow the order of operations, as usual.\n\nSo, to evaluate the expression above, first, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{6 \\div [(3 + 5) \\div 4]} + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= {6 \\div [8 \\div 4]} + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{6 \\div [8 \\div 4]} + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= {6 \\div 2} + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the operation inside the braces:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{6 \\div 2} + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 3 + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c2534ed1-72be-5d1d-a1d4-a07550442b13" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8571 :tutorial/title "Numerical Expressions With Braces"} :step/id #uuid "af52054c-35d9-57c3-98d7-5a48f6962dda" :step/math-academy-id 131207 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143647}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142782 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142597 :question/id #uuid "aa7f2efe-034e-54a9-85db-b01636f3b788" :question/math-academy-id "e-13718"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f291380e-7b67-5bd3-b5b0-1ebe6d2d9aa5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143581}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the First Operation in an Expression With Braces"} :step/id #uuid "f1026160-5385-56a6-8797-842c1e0a9527" :step/math-academy-id 131208 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143648}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142783 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142598 :question/id #uuid "d00f55c4-4a8f-5a1c-953b-007910f69203" :question/math-academy-id "e-13720"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caa497e0-947d-55bf-a026-9b665e8f8b7e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143360}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Containing Braces"} :step/id #uuid "5fc59a74-e40e-569f-af5a-bd734121e5e4" :step/math-academy-id 131211}] :activity/title "Evaluating Whole Number Expressions With Parentheses" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186143028}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060828} :activity/id #uuid "5603ad2f-6303-5415-9efb-d300f8c7daae" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048924 :topic/id #uuid "cca625de-8811-56b0-8301-4b6e2e4aa368" :topic/math-academy-id 258} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060868 :tutorial/content "We can use the standard algorithm to multiply two-digit numbers.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use the standard algorithm to compute $12\\times 13$.\n\nRecall that ${\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}3}$ in expanded form is ${\\color{blue}10} + {\\color{red}3}$. The idea is to first multiply $12$ by ${\\color{red}3}$, then by ${\\color{blue}10}$, and add the results.\n\nWe write one number above the other as follows:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}36 + 120 = 156\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the top number ($12$) by the value in the ones place of the second number ($\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{3}$), writing the result under the line:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}3; 3 \\mid 6 \\mid 12 \\cdot 3 = 36000\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply $12$ by $\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{1}0$. Let's do this in steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Any number multiplied by $10$ always gives a zero at the end. Therefore, we place a zero in the ones column underneath our first result.\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}3; 3\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}12 \\cdot 10 = 1200\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now, we work out $\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2}\\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{1}$. We place the answer next to our zero:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}3; 3\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 2 \\cdot 1 = 20\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Next, we we work out $\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{1}\\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{1}$:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}3; 3\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}2 \\mid 0 \\mid 1 \\cdot 1 = 10\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}3; 3\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}6 \\mid 36 + 120 = 156\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n12 \\times 13 = 156\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c5e81bce-1ac7-5040-8119-b2548f14f16f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1758 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c65146f8-f7f2-5175-8f4b-ccbf6a287fdc" :step/math-academy-id 5324 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060829}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060829 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059857 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059455 :question/id #uuid "fe28e925-3711-5bd4-8937-22072ba23ddf" :question/math-academy-id "e-1646"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06797272-eabc-558c-8528-aae8b6166c02" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050735}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060308}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Two-Digit Number by a Two-Digit Number"} :step/id #uuid "469e26a7-ca8d-5fb8-91ed-400f5368f8de" :step/math-academy-id 5325 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060830}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060830 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060869 :tutorial/content "Just like when multiplying a number by a one-digit number, we will sometimes have carries. Let's see how to deal with this by multiplying $24$ by $32$.\n\nWe write it this way:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}48 + 720 = 768\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n0; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2; 4 \\mid 8 \\mid 24 \\cdot 2 = 48000\n$$\n\nOn this occasion, there were no carries when multiplying the ones, and so the carry box $0$ remains empty.\n\nNext, we multiply the tens. Let's do this in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We start by writing a zero:\n\n$$\n0; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2; 4\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}24 \\cdot 30 = 7200\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: We now work out\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4}\\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{3} = {\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}2}\n$$\n\nWe carry the ${\\color{blue}1}$ and write down $\\color{red}2$:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2; 4\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 4 \\cdot 3 = 12; Carry:1; Write:2\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We multiply\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2}\\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{3} = 6\n$$\n\nand we add the carry from the previous multiplication to make\n\n$$\n6+{\\color{blue}{1}} = {\\color{red}{7}}\n$$\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2; 4\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ 7\\end{vmatrix}2 \\mid 0 \\mid 2 \\cdot 3 = 6and6 + 1 = 7; Carry: -; Write:7\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2; 4\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid 48 + 720 = 768\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n24 \\times 32 = 768\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a7fed308-698f-538a-ba9e-c52f4af74118" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1759 :tutorial/title "Multiplying Two-Digit Numbers by Two-Digit Numbers with Carries"} :step/id #uuid "2f70e43c-c28a-5ff3-817c-23c9e9a9a7e6" :step/math-academy-id 5328 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060831}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060831 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059858 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059456 :question/id #uuid "63271e65-fc04-503f-8d3c-393fbc917c44" :question/math-academy-id "e-1647"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5877b2bb-7a70-5239-8de1-dc69fd79eec2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060298}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Two-Digit Number by a Two-Digit Number With Carried Digits"} :step/id #uuid "e56edb46-4172-5ede-aeb1-40c6e064488c" :step/math-academy-id 5329 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060832}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060832 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059859 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059457 :question/id #uuid "89ef4df4-31cb-5fbc-b5cc-ef283870592a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1648"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85f7df01-47df-5ca6-bdc2-9485017b51bd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440396}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Two-Digit Number by a Two-Digit Number: Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "856610ea-9f57-51a1-9df2-7afa99daa958" :step/math-academy-id 5332}] :activity/title "Two-Digit by Two-Digit Multiplication" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059933}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074808} :activity/id #uuid "ffbb1def-7b21-5f14-bdfa-23d5d2266261" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049143 :topic/id #uuid "de38e5b7-4add-5afb-a5c0-27a0452da260" :topic/math-academy-id 536} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073785 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073657 :question/id #uuid "4e4c3088-724e-56c3-bb7b-14df1eb1327c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8107"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814eefa6-3583-5127-b0a1-6302ce674d8e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048145}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074317}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing When the Hundreds Digit is Greater Than the Divisor (Using a Template)"} :step/id #uuid "92e68259-19b3-5fab-92d9-341ce4781c8f" :step/math-academy-id 30342 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074809}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074952 :tutorial/content "We can use the standard algorithm to divide a three-digit number by a one-digit number. To illustrate, let's divide $468$ by $2$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $2$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the hundreds. How many times does $2$ go into ${\\color{red}4}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}2}$ times, and\n\n$$\n2\\times {\\color{blue}2} =4\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}2}$ above the hundreds, the $4$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the remainder of $0$. Then we bring the tens digit $(6)$ down next to the remainder.\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$2$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 2 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $2 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 4$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens. How many times does $2$ go into ${\\color{red}6}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}3}$ times, and\n\n$$\n2\\times {\\color{blue}3} =6\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}6} - 6 = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}3}$ above the tens, the $6$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the remainder of $0$. Then, we bring the ones digit $(8)$ down next to the remainder.\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}3$$2$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}6} \\div 2 = {\\color{blue}3}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $2 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}6} - 6 = 0$Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones. How many times does $2$ go into ${\\color{red}8}$? It goes in ${\\color{blue}4}$ times, and\n\n$$\n2\\times {\\color{blue}4} =8\n$$\n\nso there is a remainder of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}8} - 8 = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the ${\\color{blue}4}$ above the ones, the $8$ at the bottom, and subtract to get the final remainder of $0$.\n\n| $2$$3$$\\color{blue}4$$2$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 2 = {\\color{blue}4}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $2 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 8 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $468$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n468 \\div 2 = 234\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ffc2d3f-9be9-55c6-b804-69800505e521" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1866 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f065eaec-26f5-575c-8e95-47ec74a88b7f" :step/math-academy-id 6311 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074722}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074809 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073528 :question/id #uuid "b83956c9-d606-5ce0-bc37-c00a9498e61f" :question/math-academy-id "e-1819"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a57409-8112-5713-b2cb-85c2d9ffbae1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049577}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074291}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing When the Hundreds Digit is Greater Than the Divisor"} :step/id #uuid "02d8811c-b0a4-5fa2-97f9-fad06da89fa2" :step/math-academy-id 6312 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074810}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074810 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074953 :tutorial/content "We can also use the standard algorithm to find the value of $305 \\div 5$. However, because the hundreds digit ($3$) is less than the divisor ($5$), the process will be slightly different.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\n**Warning**: Notice that $\\color{red}3$ (the number of hundreds in ${\\color{red}3}05$) is less than the divisor $5$, so it doesn't divide. To counter this, we should consider both the hundreds *and* tens. That is, we should divide $\\color{red}30$ by $5$, as follows:\n\n| $\\color{blue}6$$5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}3} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}30} \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}6}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}6$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 30$Subtract: ${\\color{red}30} - 30 = 0$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $6$$\\color{blue}1$$5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$0$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}5} \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 5$Subtract: ${\\color{red}5} - 5 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $305$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n305 \\div 5 = 61\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d212998b-5384-5c9a-84f9-9f5780e1b8d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1867 :tutorial/title "Dividing When the Hundreds Digit Is Less Than the Divisor"} :step/id #uuid "2b90a454-8f0b-5456-8a77-9e48ff540512" :step/math-academy-id 6313 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074811}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073529 :question/id #uuid "99d7e5f9-0989-54d9-aa02-4a4a97365176" :question/math-academy-id "e-1820"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a1e476-39d5-529d-8632-c5dce93b8ed7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074305}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing When the Hundreds Digit Is Less Than the Divisor"} :step/id #uuid "9bd87e6f-f3bd-54dc-b78a-3a14a3f1b4c6" :step/math-academy-id 6314 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074954 :tutorial/content "We can also use the standard algorithm to find the value of $408 \\div 4$. We will see that the situation here is slightly different. Let's work through it.\n\nAs usual, we start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 4$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 4 = 0$Bring down: $0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}0$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$\\color{red}0$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}0} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}0}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWait! Notice that $\\color{red}0$ is less than the divisor $4$, but not all numbers of the dividend have yet been used! In this case, we need to bring down one more digit:\n\n| $1$$0$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$0$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$0$$\\color{blue}2$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$0$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $4 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 8$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 8 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $408$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n408 \\div 4 = 102\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4e2e0d2b-4bb1-5f52-8762-c80e16d26efe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1868 :tutorial/title "Cases Where We Bring Down More Than One Digit at a Time"} :step/id #uuid "b0091640-9e7c-5a24-a5fc-74683d8bd89a" :step/math-academy-id 6315 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074813}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073530 :question/id #uuid "0a15fdeb-2ae0-51d2-b35e-0e1dadde8de4" :question/math-academy-id "e-1821"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814a3ad2-3424-567e-8c74-2e1e6a75eb04" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074314}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where We Bring Down More Than One Digit at a Time"} :step/id #uuid "289d8995-9823-5fd4-83fd-0b16670d28ae" :step/math-academy-id 6316}] :activity/title "Three-Digit by One-Digit Division" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073956}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143666} :activity/id #uuid "cffad18d-56c6-5546-8bee-5c631a66739a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049253 :topic/id #uuid "e6d4682e-e5ec-5a7f-a918-a708b14b5010" :topic/math-academy-id 2378} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143666 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144091 :tutorial/content "To multiply fractions, we multiply the numerators, multiply the denominators, and simplify (if necessary).\n\nFor example, to multiply $\\dfrac 1 {2} \\times \\dfrac 1 {3},$ we multiply the numerators and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}2} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}3} =  \\dfrac {{\\color{blue}1}\\times {\\color{blue}1}} {{\\color{red}2}\\times {\\color{red}3}} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nNotice that we get the same answer when we use a fraction model.\n\n![](images/6d/6d4a0a382c9624510581d94e5829b2be6907e493aaba20eb471901f061b87820.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "a71c2faa-d009-5288-8863-51238a73c406" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8927 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "34b8df92-1cb5-5d85-b9c3-c28c62faa4f4" :step/math-academy-id 135462 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142795 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142638 :question/id #uuid "cb9425fb-6e26-5706-ada1-105bd2dbf545" :question/math-academy-id "e-14426"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "919df631-8d1d-59bf-9818-874a52f3bfe0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048962}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427991}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Fractions by Unit Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "ad2aa659-63dd-584a-b088-2ba77cc88124" :step/math-academy-id 135463 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143668}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142639 :question/id #uuid "895012bb-d418-5720-92c1-771fa03ec8b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-14430"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "476cf433-d982-5f14-89be-48aea2abaf55" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440394}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "2c4252d5-5840-55f8-ba2c-0227c7321d1a" :step/math-academy-id 135466 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142797 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142640 :question/id #uuid "c749c71d-16bc-5b50-b013-8c049618b073" :question/math-academy-id "e-14431"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "926cd557-6aff-5864-a16b-023793a1e95d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050078}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427989}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Fractions When Simplification is Needed"} :step/id #uuid "e62b1b5f-b518-59e2-b361-2cc41f5b3e7b" :step/math-academy-id 135469}] :activity/title "Multiplying Fractions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186143013}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199367} :activity/id #uuid "16dc136b-432c-5dc7-a8a2-ed7099e307f8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049640 :topic/id #uuid "077c11c6-bbf9-5f48-b091-c7882c963893" :topic/math-academy-id 4204} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199364 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198430 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198072 :question/id #uuid "98fef7ef-411f-5769-93a5-3ea615a9ab47" :question/math-academy-id "e-12542"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44fe2ae1-cc39-587f-923e-8d972a710f3b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049001}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198990}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Fractions When One Denominator Is a Multiple of the Other"} :step/id #uuid "8f9c6e33-6295-588d-8999-777f967dd926" :step/math-academy-id 45210 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199385}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199365 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198431 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198073 :question/id #uuid "875ec294-ccef-5f67-b01e-db236d47c562" :question/math-academy-id "e-12543"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf381e5b-c6ba-5255-8d6a-797576f71baf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440395}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Fractions When the Least Common Denominator Equals the Product of Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "08ad9ff4-b6a2-5573-9122-1358d801f979" :step/math-academy-id 45213 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199386}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199366 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198432 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198074 :question/id #uuid "2d2649cb-ce01-57de-b54f-b6b6d4267ac4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12544"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dba89cfa-b854-5e77-8ec3-a8a5387e1284" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050581}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198992}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Fractions When the Denominators Have a Common Factor"} :step/id #uuid "861b0ef5-57db-54f1-9e2d-753ce0381aaf" :step/math-academy-id 45216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199367 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199497 :tutorial/content "We saw how to compare fraction models using common denominators in a previous lesson. In this lesson, we'll learn how to carry out these processes without models.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}3} \\qquad \\dfrac{4}{\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\nWhich of them is larger?\n\nAt the moment, it's difficult to compare them as they don't have a common denominator. Therefore, we must *create* a common denominator.\n\nNotice that our denominators are ${\\color{blue}3}$ and ${\\color{red}{6}}$, and ${\\color{red}{6}}$ is a multiple of ${\\color{blue}3}$. In fact,\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{6}} = {\\color{purple}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can create a common denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of the *first* fraction by ${\\color{purple}{2}}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3} &= \\frac{1 \\cdot 2}{3 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{2}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now have the following two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{red}6} \\qquad \\dfrac{4}{\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\nFrom here, it's easy to see that the second fraction is larger. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{red}6} < \\dfrac{4}{\\color{red}6}\\,\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}3} < \\dfrac{4}{\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "f0d25e8e-bb9e-50d9-b3d0-fd69a9943adb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7821 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b29119b6-dd48-5ca3-b83d-aa49d38cb72e" :step/math-academy-id 45243 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199364}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199385 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199511 :tutorial/content "Consider the following fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}2} \\qquad \\dfrac{1}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nLet's compare these fractions using common denominators.\n\nNotice that ${\\color{red}3}$ is *not* a multiple of ${\\color{blue}2}$. However, we can create a common denominator by taking the product of the denominators.\n\nIn this case, our common denominator will be\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{red}3} = 6\n$$\n\n- To put $\\dfrac12$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{red}3}{:}$\n$\\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1 \\cdot 3}{2 \\cdot 3} = \\frac{3}{6}$\n- To put $\\dfrac13$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}2}{:}$\n$\\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1 \\cdot 2}{3 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{2}{6}$\n\nSo, we now have the following two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac36 \\qquad\\dfrac26\n$$\n\nFrom here, it's easy to see that the first fraction is larger. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac36 > \\dfrac26\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12 > \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "6d0d99a2-4c43-5268-82e8-cb9f2c133f94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7921 :tutorial/title "Cases Where the Least Common Denominator Is a Product of the Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "ff41765d-5768-5be7-b7c9-fc631dd1a47b" :step/math-academy-id 45627 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199365}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199386 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199512 :tutorial/content "Whenever two denominators have a common factor greater than $1$, the most efficient way to find a least common denominator is to look at the denominators' multiples.\n\nFor example, consider the following fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}6} \\qquad \\dfrac{5}{\\color{red}9}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${\\color{blue}6}$ and ${\\color{red}9}$ have $3$ as a common factor. This suggests that we can create a common denominator by looking at their least common multiples:\n\n- Multiples of $6{:}\\qquad 6,12,{\\color{purple}{18}},24,\\ldots$\n- Multiples of $9{:}\\qquad 9,{\\color{purple}{18}},27,36,\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, our least common denominator will be ${\\color{purple}{18}}$.\n\n- To put $\\dfrac26$ over a denominator of $18$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n$\\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{6 \\cdot 3} = \\frac{6}{18}$\n- To put $\\dfrac59$ over a denominator of $18$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$\\frac{5}{9} = \\frac{5 \\cdot 2}{9 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{10}{18}$\n\nSo, we now have the following two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{18} \\qquad\\dfrac{10}{18}\n$$\n\nFrom here, it's easy to see that the second fraction is larger. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{18}< \\dfrac{10}{18}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac26 < \\dfrac59\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "5537959f-5b04-5412-93ab-5f1fde21a8f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7922 :tutorial/title "Using Lowest Common Multiples"} :step/id #uuid "f04271e1-d9ba-5ac2-8cbf-99e19cd76ae0" :step/math-academy-id 45628 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199366}}] :activity/title "Comparing Fractions Using Common Denominators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198504}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074782} :activity/id #uuid "90922140-3342-5248-9673-d547d47f6e31" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049752 :topic/id #uuid "10fd490a-3c5a-5c83-890f-fe489da02ca5" :topic/math-academy-id 545} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074782 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074949 :tutorial/content "The method of subtracting fractions and whole numbers is very similar to the one we use for addition.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the value of\n\n$$\n2-\\dfrac{5}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nWe first write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}6$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{6}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2 \\times {\\color{blue}{6}}}{1\\times{\\color{blue}{6}}} = \\dfrac{12}{6}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{12}{6}}_{2} - \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same.\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{6} - \\frac{5}{6} = \\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 {6}\n$$\n\nWe can always check our answer using a fraction model:\n\n![](images/e6/e67c582daf0ab40f08c2445b1f0527e4316d61114d12ce1aa56ede6c26f3b3a1.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "833f94cb-00c4-54ac-9a66-7eaa2c22de12" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1822 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f81659bc-1892-5609-9c60-42dac412c41a" :step/math-academy-id 5812 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074783}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073824 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073521 :question/id #uuid "c5191ff9-e7ec-5998-a0a9-a0f2b2d41d76" :question/math-academy-id "e-1743"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814eed58-63c9-5c76-8755-4f2acaaff494" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050462}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074324}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Fractions and Whole Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "25edebc1-bba6-5b8b-b3b5-95d970debf8b" :step/math-academy-id 5813 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074784}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074784 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073825 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073522 :question/id #uuid "d566c6a3-426d-591e-a884-16ff5e10a825" :question/math-academy-id "e-1745"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc470d59-c192-545c-b748-0fb5601fabbb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074309}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Fractions and Whole Numbers: Converting the Final Answer to a Mixed Number"} :step/id #uuid "3db01d60-ca05-5013-9ada-8f37116562b7" :step/math-academy-id 5817 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074785}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073523 :question/id #uuid "f6f05542-54f4-5fc6-807b-b012abcfaa70" :question/math-academy-id "e-1747"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee87e73-843c-5596-b1f2-341d4e7266f8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440393}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Final Answer to a Mixed Number: Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "31de2e66-5c85-5777-b47a-a391d926274b" :step/math-academy-id 5821}] :activity/title "Subtracting Fractions and Whole Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073965}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074571} :activity/id #uuid "fa246678-7839-53ff-9386-c4853b180cc3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050462 :topic/id #uuid "4b8931aa-9315-57e3-a2df-c03148898585" :topic/math-academy-id 539} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075056 :tutorial/content "It's straightforward to find the sum of a whole number and a proper fraction when we want the final answer to be a mixed number.\n\nFor instance, we can add $6$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}6} + {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{5}} = {\\color{red}6}\\,{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{5}}\n$$\n\nOkay, that was pretty easy. And notice that we didn't need a fraction model to do it.\n\nThere are other ways to add whole numbers and fractions, which we'll discuss shortly. But first, let's see a few examples of this idea." :tutorial/id #uuid "c8501633-3a6b-5553-8f1c-c52dc60cfe15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8973 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0abc657a-002d-5ac6-8496-0e5415ffbebc" :step/math-academy-id 116987 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073501 :question/id #uuid "081630fd-f068-5eee-8d6f-89f871daabf7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14509"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16f0ec0-6c22-501b-90cd-9eb65b050aee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048907}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074303}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Sum as a Mixed Number"} :step/id #uuid "d111996f-eb5f-57a8-a848-d8ebf6522915" :step/math-academy-id 116988 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074573}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075057 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to add a whole number and a fraction. If we want the final answer to be an improper fraction, then we can proceed by turning the whole number into an improper fraction.\n\nFor example, let's find\n\n$$\n2+\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction.\n\nWe start by writing $2$ as an improper fraction with $1$ in the denominator:\n\n$$\n2 =\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}5$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}5}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\times {\\color{blue}{5}}}{1\\times {\\color{blue}{5}}} = \\dfrac{10}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can add the two numbers:\n\n$$\n2 + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{10}{5}_(⏟)_(2) + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{11}{5}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nTo use some math jargon, we put the numbers $2$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nover a **common denominator** of ${\\color{blue}5}$ before adding them.\n\nNotice that this process is very similar to using fraction models.\n\n![](images/cb/cbe49aa64f7d53911592001c5d93e8cde826ac46dabf2ba9c5e9579738ce6ebd.png)\n\nTo add $2$ to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nusing a model, we split each whole into ${\\color{blue}5}$ equal parts. When we do, we see that there are $11$ shaded parts in total. So we arrive at the same answer:\n\n$$\n2 +\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5} = \\dfrac{11}{{\\color{blue}5}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cf351f38-5bcb-5302-93d1-22730dbb7ef1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8974 :tutorial/title "Adding Proper Fractions and Whole Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "ea2f4743-4aac-5c5c-9b9b-7bd499491aaa" :step/math-academy-id 116991 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073502 :question/id #uuid "ba9e10b8-4601-5f2a-8681-0ab478bdb081" :question/math-academy-id "e-14511"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa913057-6bdb-5708-b094-da7880696db4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050311}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074302}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Sum as an Improper Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "4252d06c-1791-5c1d-a068-07b9fbfc75c7" :step/math-academy-id 116992 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075058 :tutorial/content "How do we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{\\color{blue}4} + 5\n$$\n\nas a mixed number?\n\nNotice that we cannot write\n\n$$\n5\\,\\dfrac 7 4\n$$\n\nbecause\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 4\n$$\n\nis an improper fraction. Instead, let's find this sum using the same method as before.\n\nFirst, we write $5$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}4$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{4}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5 \\times {\\color{blue}{4}}}{1 \\times{\\color{blue}{4}}} = \\dfrac{20}{4}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find the following sum:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{4} + \\dfrac{20}{4}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{4} + \\frac{20}{4} = \\frac{7 + 20}{4} = \\frac{27}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert this to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n27 \\div 4 = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 3 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c2a88636-00da-54bc-b8d0-f5c0289bf1ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8976 :tutorial/title "Adding Improper Fractions and Whole Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "251c325e-a7d6-5c8a-973c-2664e56b18ff" :step/math-academy-id 116995 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073698 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073503 :question/id #uuid "6247fce4-259e-5569-bbdf-b88cc1ea9482" :question/math-academy-id "e-14515"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7bb96c-c682-54da-98d7-aa1b6960ee9d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050249}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440397}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Improper Fraction and a Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "9a4687be-5cd4-572a-bbb5-d8859631f746" :step/math-academy-id 116996}] :activity/title "Adding Fractions and Whole Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073959}]}
